our points no three are collinear with an admissible slope, so each sunny line can cover at most two points, leaving at least one point uncovered.  \n     - If it contains a single point, the two sunny lines would need to cover five points, which is impossible for the same reason (each sunny line can cover at most two of those points).\n\n4. In every case we reach a contradiction; therefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\nThus the answer to the explicit task is that the required configuration is **impossible**.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on at least one of the three distinct lines\n\\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).  \nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nWe assume, for contradiction, that exactly two of the three lines are sunny.\nHence one line, say \\(L_{1}\\), is non‑sunny; i.e. \\(L_{1}\\) is parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary observation\n\nFor any subset \\(Q\\subseteq P\\) the following holds:\n\n*If three points of \\(Q\\) are collinear, then the line through them has slope\n\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).*\n\nIndeed, the six points of \\(P\\) have \\(y\\)-coordinates \\(1,2,3\\) and\n\\(x\\)-coordinates \\(1,2,3\\); the only possible equalities among them are\n\\(y= \\text{const}\\), \\(x=\\text{const}\\) or \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).  \nA direct check of all \\(\\binom{6}{3}=20\\) triples confirms that any three\ncollinear points of \\(P\\) lie on a line of one of those three forbidden\ndirections.\n\nConsequently, **any sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(P\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Case analysis for the non‑sunny line \\(L_{1}\\)\n\n#### Case A: \\(L_{1}\\) is horizontal, \\(y=c\\).\n\nThe possible values of \\(c\\) are \\(1,2,3\\).  \n\n*If \\(L_{1}\\) contains three points* (e.g. \\(c=1\\) gives \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\)),\nthe remaining three points are \\((1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\).  \nAny line through two of them is either vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); thus\nboth remaining lines would be non‑sunny, contradicting the hypothesis that\nexactly one line is non‑sunny.\n\n*If \\(L_{1}\\) contains at most two points*, then at least four points of \\(P\\)\nmust be covered by the two sunny lines \\(L_{2},L_{3}\\).  \nSince each sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(P\\) (by the\npreliminary observation), the only way to cover four points is that each\nsunny line contains exactly two points.  \nA straightforward inspection of all pairs of points shows that the only\npairs that can be joined by a sunny line are\n\\((1,2)-(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) and \\((2,1)-(1,3)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).\nThese two lines together cover the four points\n\\((1,2),(3,1),(2,1),(1,3)\\) and leave \\((2,2)\\) uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nHence the horizontal case is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case B: \\(L_{1}\\) is vertical, \\(x=c\\).\n\nThe argument is symmetric to Case A (swap the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)).\nExactly the same contradictions arise; therefore a vertical non‑sunny line\ncannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case C: \\(L_{1}\\) is diagonal, \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nThe possible sums are \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n*If \\(L_{1}\\) contains three points* (the only possibility is \\(c=4\\),\ngiving \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\)), the remaining points are\n\\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nAny line through two of these three points is either vertical or has slope\n\\(-1\\); thus at least one of the remaining lines would be non‑sunny, again a\ncontradiction.\n\n*If \\(L_{1}\\) contains two points*, the only such diagonal is \\(x+y=3\\