over all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   Consequently \\(k=1\\) is also realizable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nThese two constructions settle the existence of configurations with exactly \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) sunny lines for any admissible \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the family of vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is **not** sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and none of them is sunny.  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) except the point \\((n,1)\\), because the only points with \\(x=n\\) are \\((n,1)\\).\n\nChoose any other point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); for definiteness we use \\((1,2)\\).  \nThe line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y-1 = \\frac{1-2}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n)\n      = -\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\), which is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nTherefore \\(S\\) is a **sunny** line.\n\nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1},S\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(S\\)) is sunny, and together they contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions showing that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\) is attainable (all lines vertical), and  \n* \\(k=1\\) is attainable (all but one line vertical, the remaining line sunny).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) certainly contains \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\u220e"

