st one point to each of\n   those \\(t\\) columns, the whole family of \\(t\\) non‑vertical lines can cover\n   at most \\(t\\cdot t=t^{2}\\) points of the set \\(\\bigcup_{c=v+1}^{n}C_c\\).\n   Hence we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   S_t\\le t^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Using (1) this inequality becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{t(t+1)}2\\le t^{2}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad t\\ge 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality is always true, but it is *sharp*: equality holds only for\n   \\(t=1\\).  Indeed, if \\(t\\ge 2\\) then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{t(t+1)}2 > t^{2}\\quad\\text{(since }t(t+1)-2t^{2}=t-2>0\\text{)} ,\n   \\]\n\n   which contradicts the covering requirement.  Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\le 1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad v\\ge n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   In words: **at least \\(n-1\\) of the \\(n\\) lines must be vertical.**\n\n4. **Consequences for sunny lines.**  \n   A sunny line is, by definition, not vertical.  Hence the number of sunny\n   lines is at most the number of non‑vertical lines, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le t\\le 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(k\\) can be only \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).\n\n5. **Realisation of the two possibilities.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence the point\n   lies on \\(L_a\\).  All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1),\n   \\]\n\n   which cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) except \\((n,1)\\).\n   Add the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\):\n\n   \\[\n   S:\\;y-1=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n)\n   \\qquad\\bigl(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac1{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1},S\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   contains exactly one sunny line, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at\n   least one of