bseteq\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) such that each point of\n\\(S_n\\) is incident to at least one member of \\(X\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{x\\mid (x,y)\\in S_n\\}=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\nB=\\{y\\mid (x,y)\\in S_n\\}=A,\n\\]\n\\[\nC=\\{x+y\\mid (x,y)\\in S_n\\}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|A|=|B|=n\\) and \\(|C|=n\\).  \nA line from \\(\\mathcal H\\) can cover at most one element of \\(B\\); a line from\n\\(\\mathcal V\\) can cover at most one element of \\(A\\); a line from \\(\\mathcal D\\)\ncan cover at most one element of \\(C\\).\n\nConsequently, if we pick fewer than \\(n\\) lines from the union\n\\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\), at least one element of one of the\nsets \\(A,B,C\\) remains uncovered, and the corresponding lattice point of\n\\(S_n\\) is uncovered as well.  This is a direct application of Hall’s marriage\ntheorem to the bipartite incidence graph between the points of \\(S_n\\) and the\nthree families.\n\nFrom (2) we have selected only \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. fewer than \\(n\\).\nTherefore some point of \\(S_n\\) is not covered – a contradiction.\nHence **no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists** for any\n\\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for all other values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq 2\\), a\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and\ncovering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{vertical lines }x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\\\\n&\\text{horizontal line }y=1,\\\\\n&\\text{diagonal line }x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\( (n-1)+1+1=n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n* The vertical lines cover every point whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at most \\(n-1\\).\n* The horizontal line covers the points with \\(y=1\\) that are not already covered.\n* The diagonal line covers the points on the hypotenuse \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the three groups, so the\ncovering condition holds and \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sun}}:\\; y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.\nIt passes through the points \\((t,t)\\) for \\(1\\le t\\le n\\), thereby covering\nall points on the main diagonal that are not already covered by the vertical\nlines.  Together the \\(n\\) lines cover the whole set \\(S_n\\), giving a\nconfiguration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The case \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=n-m\\) where \\(0\\le m\\le n-3\\).  \nWe shall use \\(m\\) vertical non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nThe remaining points have \\(x\\)-coordinates in the interval\n\\(\\{m+1,m+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Their number is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}2 = \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\ge 3\\), we can partition this set into \\(k\\) disjoint subsets,\neach containing exactly two points, and draw a distinct sunny line through each\npair.  A convenient explicit partition is obtained by pairing, for each\n\\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nP_i=\\bigl\\{(m+i,\\;i),\\;(n,\\;i+(n-m-1)\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth points of \\(P_i\\) belong to \\(S_n\\) (their sums are \\(m+2i\\le n+1\\) and\n\\(n+i+(n-m-1)=2n-m+i-1\\le n+1\\) because \\(i\\le k=n-m\\)).  \nThe line through the two points of \\(P_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i+(n-m-1)-i}{\\,n-(m+i)}=\\frac{n-m-1}{\\,n-m-i}= \\frac{k-1}{k-i},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a rational number different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore the\nline is sunny.  Distinct values of \\(i\\) give distinct slopes, so the \\(k\\) lines\nare pairwise different.\n\nEvery point with \\(x\\ge m+1\\) belongs to exactly one of the pairs \\(P_i\\), hence\nlies on one of the sunny lines.  Consequently the \\(m\\) vertical lines (3) together\nwith the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover all of \\(S_n\\), and the total number of lines\nis \\(m+k=n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Exhaustion of possibilities\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* No configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists (Section 1).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2\\) a configuration\n  satisfying the required covering property **does** exist (Sections 2.1–2.3).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
