   L_{n}&: y=n x-(n-1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   All slopes are positive integers \\(\\ge 1\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  \n   Set\n   \\[\n   t:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\Bigr\\rceil\\qquad\\text{(with the convention }t=1\\text{ when }a=1).\n   \\]\n   Because \\(b-1\\le n-a\\) we obtain \\(1\\le t\\le n\\).  \n   A direct computation shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\):\n   \\[\n   b = t a-(t-1).\n   \\]\n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\((1)\\).  \n   Thus for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **Increasing the number of sunny lines one by one.**  \n   Starting from the configuration of all non‑sunny diagonals \\(\\{D_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) we replace diagonals by sunny lines while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \n   For each integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) we construct a configuration with exactly \\(r\\) sunny lines as follows.\n\n   *Step \\(r\\) (induction on \\(r\\)).*  \n   Assume we already have a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{r-1}\\) with \\(r-1\\) sunny lines and \\(n-(r-1)\\) non‑sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).  \n   Choose a non‑sunny diagonal \\(D_j\\) that is present in \\(\\mathcal C_{r-1}\\).  \n   Replace \\(D_j\\) by the sunny line\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\; y=jx-(j-1) .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   (Notice that \\(L_j\\) is exactly the line \\(L_j\\) from the family \\((1)\\).)  \n\n   The points of \\(D_j\\) are \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=j+1\\}\\).  \n   For every such point we have \\(b=j+1-a\\), whence\n   \\[\n   b = j a-(j-1) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+b=j+1 .\n   \\]\n   Thus **every** point of the diagonal \\(D_j\\) also lies on the sunny line \\(L_j\\).  \n   Consequently the replacement does not destroy the covering property, the total number of lines stays \\(n\\), and the number of sunny lines increases by one.  \n\n   Repeating this replacement \\(r\\) times we obtain a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{r}\\) with exactly \\(r\\) sunny lines (the other \\(n-r\\) lines are the still‑unused diagonals).  \n\n   Because the replacement works for any diagonal, we can realise **every** integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n\\) provided the replacement does not run out of diagonals.  \n   For \\(r=n\\) we have already given the explicit family \\((1)\\); for \\(0\\le r\\le n-1\\) the inductive construction above yields the required configuration.\n\n4. **The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2).\n   \\]\n   *Existence of \\(k=0,1,3\\).*  \n   – \\(k=0\\): the three diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) cover all six points.  \n   – \\(k=1\\): the three lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=x\\) (the first two are non‑sunny, the third is sunny) cover the set.  \n   – \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines\n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+ \\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n     \\]\n     each pass through two of the required points and together cover all six points.\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   Because there are only three lines in total, exactly one line is non‑sunny; it must be one of the three diagonals, say \\(x+y=4\\), which contains the four points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(1,3)\\).  \n   The remaining three points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   Any line through two of these three points has slope either \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)).  \n   All three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line, so the three points cannot be covered by only two sunny lines.  \n   Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n   Consequently for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\nCombining the constructions f