 The claimed all‑sunny construction for \\(n\\ge4\\)  \n> “For \\(n\\ge4\\) we exhibit \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that cover \\(S_n\\):\n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> L_1&: y=x,\\\\\n> L_2&: y=2x-1,\\\\\n> L_3&: y=3x-2,\\\\\n> &\\ \\vdots\\\\\n> L_{n}&: y=n x-(n-1).\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]”\n\n**Issue Identified:** **Critical Error**.  \n**Explanation:**  \n- All listed lines have slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\), none of which are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus they are indeed sunny.  \n- However, the claim that these \\(n\\) lines **cover** every point of \\(S_n\\) is false.  \n  - For any point with \\(a=1\\) and \\(b>1\\) (e.g. \\((1,2)\\)), substituting \\(x=1\\) into any line of the form \\(y= i x-(i-1)\\) yields \\(y=1\\). Hence all lines intersect the vertical line \\(x=1\\) at the single point \\((1,1)\\). Consequently, points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) are **not** covered.  \n  - Direct computation in the next paragraph (see below) incorrectly asserts that every \\((a,b)\\) satisfies one of the equations, which is not the case.  \n\nThus the construction does **not** satisfy the covering requirement, invalidating the subsequent argument that \\(k=n\\) is attainable for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n### 4. The “direct computation’’ that purports to verify the covering  \n> “Set \\(t:=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\bigr\\rceil\\) (with the convention \\(t=1\\) when \\(a=1\\)).  \n> Because \\(b-1\\le n-a\\) we obtain \\(1\\le t\\le n\\).  \n> A direct computation shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\):\n> \\[\n> b = t a-(t-1).\n> \\]”\n\n**Issue Identified:** **Critical Error**.  \n**Explanation:**  \n- The equality \\(b = t a-(t-1)\\) is equivalent to \\((b-1) = t (a-1)\\). By definition of \\(t\\), we have \\(t-1 < \\frac{b-1}{a-1} \\le t\\). Multiplying by \\(a-1>0\\) gives \\((t-1)(a-1) < b-1 \\le t(a-1)\\).  \n- The left inequality only guarantees \\((b-1) > (t-1)(a-1)\\); it does **not** force \\((b-1) = t(a-1)\\). Equality holds only when \\(\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\) is an integer, i.e. when \\((b-1)\\) is a multiple of \\((a-1)\\). For many points (e.g. \\((2,3)\\) where \\((b-1)/(a-1)=2\\) works, but \\((2,2)\\) gives \\(1\\) which works, while \\((3,2)\\) gives \\((b-1)/(a-1)=\\frac{1}{2}\\) and the equality fails).  \n- Moreover, for \\(a=1\\) the definition forces \\(t=1\\), yielding the equation \\(b=1\\), which is false for any \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b>1\\). Hence the claimed “direct computation’’ is incorrect, and the covering claim collapses.\n\n### 5. Increasing the number of sunny lines one by one (replacement argument)  \n> “Choose a non‑sunny diagonal \\(D_j\\) that is present in \\(\\mathcal C_{r-1}\\).  \n> Replace \\(D_j\\) by the sunny line \\(L_j:\\; y=jx-(j-1)\\).  \n> The points of \\(D_j\\) are \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=j+1\\}\\).  \n> For every such point we have \\(b=j+1-a\\), whence  \n> \\[\n> b = j a-(j-1) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+b=j+1 .\n> \\]  \n> Thus **every** point of the diagonal \\(D_j\\) also lies on the sunny line \\(L_j\\).”\n\n**Issue Identified:** **Critical Error**.  \n**Explanation:**  \n- The equivalence written is algebraically incorrect. Starting from \\(b=j+1-a\\) we obtain \\(b = -a + (j+1)\\). The right‑hand side of the claimed equivalence, \\(b = j a-(j-1)\\), simplifies to \\(b = j(a-1)+1\\). Setting these equal gives \\(-a + (j+1) = j(a-1)+1\\), which reduces to \\(-a + j + 1 = ja - j + 1\\) → \\(-a + j = ja - j\\) → \\(j + j = a(j+1)\\) → \\(2j = a(j+1)\\). This holds only for very specific integer pairs \\((a,j)\\), not for all points on the diagonal.  \n- In fact, solving the two equations simultaneously yields at most one common point (if any). For example, with \\(j=2\\) the diagonal is \\(x+y=3\\) (points \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)), while the line \\(L_2\\) is \\(y=2x-1\\); substituting \\((1,2)\\) gives \\(2=2\\cdot1-1=1\\), false, and \\((2,1)\\) gives \\(1=2\\cdot2-1=3\\), false. Hence **no** point of \\(D_2\\) lies on \\(L_2\\).  \n- Consequently, replacing a diagonal by the corresponding \\(L_j\\) destroys the covering property; the inductive construction that purports to increase the number of sunny lines one by one is invalid.\n\n### 6. Consequence for the range of admissible \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)  \n> “Because the replacement works for any diagonal, we can realise **every** integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n\\) … For \\(r=n\\) we have already given the explicit family (1).”\n\n**Issue Identified:** **Critical Error** (propagation).  \n**Explanation:** The claim that the replacement works is false (see point 5). Moreover, the explicit family (1) does not cover \\(S_n\\) (see point 3). Therefore the conclusion that any \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable for \\(n\\ge4\\) is unsupported and, in fact, false (e.g. for \\(n=4\\) the value \\(k=4\\) is impossible because each sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_4\\), so four lines can cover at most eight points, whereas \\(|S_4|=10\\)). Hence the stated admissible set for \\(n\\ge4\\) is incorrect.\n\n### 7. The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\n#### 7.1. Existence of \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)  \n> “\\(k=0\\): the three diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) cover all six points.  \n> \\(k=1\\): the three lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=x\\) (the first two are non‑sunny, the third is sunny) cover the set.”\n\n**Verification:** Both constructions are correct. The three diagonals are non‑sunny and together contain all six points of \\(S_3\\). The set \\(\\{x=1,\\,y=1,\\,y=x\\}\\) consists of two non‑sunny lines (vertical and horizontal) and one sunny line (slope 1); each of the six points lies on at least one of these lines, so \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n#### 7.2. Claim of existence for \\(k=3\\)  \n> “\\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+ \\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n> \\]  \n> each pass through two of the required points and together cover all six points.”\n\n**Issue Identified:** **Critical Error**.  \n**Explanation:**  \n- The line \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) (since \\(-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac32=1\\)) but does **not** pass through any other point of \\(S_3\\). In particular, it does not contain \\((1,2)\\) or \\((2,1)\\).  \n- The correct sunny line that covers \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) is \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\), intercept \\(\\tfrac52\\)). With this correction the three lines \\(y=x\\), \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\), and \\(y=-2x+5\\) indeed cover all six points. However, the solution presents the wrong line, so the stated configuration does **not** satisfy the covering requirement. Hence the argument for \\(k=3\\) is invalid as written.\n\n#### 7.3. Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)  \n> “Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n> Because there are only three lines in total, exactly one line is non‑sunny; it must be one of the three diagonals, say \\(x+y=4\\), which contains the four points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(1,3)\\).  \n> The remaining three points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n> Any line through two of these three points has slope either \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)).  \n> All three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line, so the three points cannot be covered by only two sunny lines.  \n> Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible when \\(n=3\\).”\n\n**Issue Identified:** **Justification Gap**.  \n**Explanation:**  \n- The diagonal \\(x+y=4\\) actually contains **three** distinct points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\); the solution mistakenly lists a fourth point \\((1,3)\\) twice. This is a minor factual slip that does not affect the core argument.  \n- The remainder of the reasoning (that the three leftover points cannot be covered by two sunny lines because any line through two of them has a forbidden slope) is correct and sufficiently rigorous. Nonetheless, the miscount indicates a lack of careful verification, which we record as a justification gap.\n\n### 8. Final statement  \n> “Combining the constructions for \\(n\\ge4\\) with the special analysis for \\(n=3\\) yields the complete description stated at the beginning.”\n\n**Issue Identified:** **Critical Error** (cumulative).  \n**Explanation:** The constructions for \\(n\\ge4\\) are invalid (see points 3–6), so the final conclusion that the admissible set of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for all \\(n\\ge4\\) is unsupported and false. Consequently the overall answer is incorrect.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution fails to provide a correct construction for the case \\(n\\ge4\\); the central “all‑sunny’’ family does not cover the required points, and the inductive replacement argument is mathematically incorrect. Additionally, the example for \\(k=3\\) when \\(n=3\\) is erroneous, and there is a minor miscount in the impossibility argument for \\(k=2\\). Because the main result relies on these flawed arguments, the solution does **not** correctly solve the problem."
