s\n   \\[\n   (t-2)(a-1)+1 < b \\le (t-1)(a-1)+1 .\n   \\]\n   Since both \\(b\\) and \\((t-1)(a-1)+1\\) are integers, the right‑hand inequality forces\n   \\[\n   b = (t-1)(a-1)+1 = t a-(t-1) .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the line \\(L_t\\) from (★).  \n\n   In both cases the point belongs to one of the lines in (★); therefore the family (★) covers the whole set \\(S_n\\).  \n   Because there are exactly \\(n\\) lines, we have produced a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n5. **The exceptional small values of \\(n\\).**  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  \n   The six points of \\(S_3\\) are\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2).\n   \\]\n   Direct inspection yields the following possibilities:\n   * \\(k=0\\): the three diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,4\\);\n   * \\(k=1\\): the three lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=x\\);\n   * \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines\n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n     \\]\n   No configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists: if two lines are sunny, the third line must be non‑sunny, hence a diagonal. A diagonal contains four points of \\(S_3\\); the remaining two points are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (or a symmetric pair). Any line through two of the remaining points has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), which is forbidden for a sunny line. Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  \n   The ten points of \\(S_4\\) are easily listed.  \n   Using the constructions of Sections 4.1–4.3 we obtain configurations with \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  \n   The case \\(k=4\\) is impossible. Indeed, a sunny line cannot be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); consequently any sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_4\\). With four sunny lines we could cover at most \\(8\\) points, whereas \\(|S_4|=10\\). Hence \\(k=4\\) cannot be realised.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the explicit constructions for the attainable values of \\(k\\) with the impossibility arguments for the missing values